---
functor: 1
title: Math basics
---

set heading(numbering: none)

Inline math is between two dollar signs: `$x + y$`. Display math is between two lines of `$$`. Math uses names, not backslashes.

# Names and spaces

A space separates two names. `d x` is d times x. For the differential, write `dd x`. A word that is not a name shows as single letters, with a warning and a hint.

| Write | Result | Note |
|:--|:-:|:--|
| `a b c` | $a b c$ | three letters |
| `2 x + 1` | $2 x + 1$ | numbers are upright |
| `3.14` | $3.14$ | a decimal point |
| `sin x` | $sin x$ | a function name is upright |
| `alpha + Omega` | $alpha + Omega$ | Greek |
| `x in A` | $x in A$ | a relation |

Single letters are italic. The signs `+ - * = < > , ; : ! / |` work as they are, and `-` is a true minus sign.

# Scripts and primes

| Write | Result |
|:--|:-:|
| `x_1`, `x^2` | $x_1, x^2$ |
| `x_(i + 1)^(n - 1)` | $x_(i + 1)^(n - 1)$ |
| `a_(i j)` | $a_(i j)$ |
| `f'`, `f''`, `f'''` | $f', f'', f'''$ |
| `f'_n`, `f''(x)` | $f'_n, f''(x)$ |
| `f'^2`, `y''^(n)_1` | $f'^2, y''^(n)_1$ |
| `e^(i pi)` | $e^(i pi)$ |

A script is one item or a group in round brackets. Two `_` on one term is an error: put both parts in one group. A prime has full size and sits at the height of the font, before the other scripts. A prime and a `^` script go together: `f'^2`.

# Groups and brackets

Round brackets group and show. `x_(i + 1)` uses the brackets as a group only. `(a + b)^2` shows them. Square and curly brackets also show.

| Write | Result |
|:--|:-:|
| `(a + b)^2` | $(a + b)^2$ |
| `[0, 1]` | $[0, 1]$ |
| `{x}` | ${x}$ |
| `(frac(a, b))` | $(frac(a, b))$ |

Brackets grow with the content. A square bracket can close with a round one, and a round one with a square one, for half-open intervals: `[0, 1)`, `(a, b]`. Groups can go 48 levels deep in one formula. A group can take operations: `(a + b).bar`, `(F_T).box`. See [Recipes](symbols/recipes.html).

# Text in math

Text in quotes is upright: `"if"`, `"tr" A`. Text gets a word space on each side, so `p "prime"` shows as $p "prime"$. There is no space next to a bracket or a comma. Use `quad` for a larger gap.

```fn
$f(x) = 0 quad "for all" quad x in R.bb$
```

# Limits

`sum`, `prod`, `union`, `inter`, `lim`, `max` and `min` put their scripts below and above in display math, and at the side in text. `integral` puts them at the side.

```fn
$$
sum_(k = 1)^n k = frac(n (n + 1), 2),   lim_(n to infty) (1 + frac(1, n))^n = e,   integral_0^1 x^2 dd x = frac(1, 3)
$$
```

## Place of the limits

One rule changes the place of the limits for one operator in the whole document. `over` puts them below and above in display math. `side` puts them at the side.

```fn
set integral(limits: over)
set sum(limits: side)

$$
integral_0^1 f(x) dd x = sum_(k = 1)^n a_k
$$
```

The rule works on the big operators (`sum`, `prod`, `coprod`, `integral`, `union`, `inter`) and on the functions with limits, such as `lim`, `max` and `det`. In text, the limits always go at the side.

## Many conditions

In a script group, `;` starts a new condition. In display math, each condition has its own line. In text, the conditions stay on one line with commas. You write the source one time.

```fn
$$
f(n) = sum_(p "prime"; p le n; p mid.not m) 1/p
$$
```

In text: $sum_(p "prime"; p le n) 1/p$.

## Wide limits

A wide limit can go under the symbols next to the operator when they do not go down into the space of the limit. Then the gap stays small. A deep neighbour, such as a `frac` or a second `sum`, keeps the full gap, so the two never touch.

`set limits(overhang: off)` keeps each limit inside the width of its operator, for all operators in the document.

```fn
$$
x = sum_(1 le i lt j le n) a_i b_j,   y = sum_(1 le i lt j le n) frac(a_i, b_j)
$$
```

## Differentials

`dd` is the differential d. It puts a thin space before the d.

```fn
$$
integral_0^1 f(x) dd x,   frac(dd y, dd x),   integral.x2_D g dd x dd y
$$
```

`set dd(style: up)` makes every `dd` upright.

